description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/solicitation-outcome-scan.ts, emit a substrateGap when operator escalations go unanswered, so the backlog is a self-report instead of something an operator must enumerate by hand.

WHY. This resolver already counts answered versus unanswered solicitations and returns them in solicitationOutcomeReport — and then tells nobody. Measured 2026-09-14: 175 solicitations, 3 answered, 172 unanswered, and the most recent human disposition before today was 2026-08-29, a sixteen day silence. Nothing in the registry is a backlog detector, so the only way to discover the queue is to resolve this shape and count the outcomes yourself. That matters more than it sounds: per escalation-disposition-apply.ts the category seal in gap-to-feature.ts has exactly one designed escape, a human verb, so every unanswered escalation is a whole gap family that can never be reattempted. Three of the unanswered ones were findings an operator re-derived from scratch this session because nobody read the queue. A gap makes the backlog visible in the same store the operator already reads.

THE CHANGE. Exactly one op. Find this line, which occurs exactly once in the file:

  return { shape: "solicitationOutcomeReport", body: { authored: true, outcomes, answered, pending: outcomes.filter((o) => o.outcome === "pending").length, episode_count: episodes === null ? 0 : episodes.length, horizon_ms: horizon } };

Replace that single line with exactly these lines:

  const unanswered = outcomes.filter((o) => o.outcome !== "answered");
  if (unanswered.length >= 20) {
    const waiting = unanswered.slice(0, 6).map((o) => o.solicitation_id).join(", ");
    const detail = "The substrate is asking for operator decisions and not getting them: " + unanswered.length + " of " + outcomes.length + " solicitations are unanswered. A sealed gap category's only designed escape is a human verb, so each unanswered escalation is a gap family that can never be reattempted. To answer one: write uiFeedback_write with panel_id set to the solicitation id and the verb in pointer.value, then resolve escalation_disposition_apply. Waiting longest: " + waiting;
    try {
      await fetch(process.env["DEV_VESSEL_IMPULSES_URL"] ?? "http://127.0.0.1:8090/v2/impulses/resolve", {
        method: "POST",
        headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
        body: JSON.stringify({ impulse: { type: "substrateGap_write", gap: { id: "operator-escalation-backlog", category: "systematic_failure", source: "substrate_detected", summary: detail, detected_at: new Date().toISOString(), status: "open", classification_metadata: { unanswered_count: unanswered.length, total_solicitations: outcomes.length, answered_count: answered } } } }),
        signal: AbortSignal.timeout(8000),
      });
    } catch { /* advisory only: the report must never fail because gap emission failed */ }
  }
  return { shape: "solicitationOutcomeReport", body: { authored: true, outcomes, answered, unanswered: unanswered.length, pending: outcomes.filter((o) => o.outcome === "pending").length, episode_count: episodes === null ? 0 : episodes.length, horizon_ms: horizon } };

WHAT THIS CHANGES AT RUNTIME. This is not cosmetic. Today resolving this shape returns counts and writes nothing, so a growing operator backlog is invisible unless somebody resolves the shape and tallies the outcomes by hand. Afterwards, whenever twenty or more solicitations are unanswered, one substrateGap with the stable id operator-escalation-backlog is upserted carrying the counts and the instructions for answering, and the report gains an unanswered field. The gap id is deliberately stable so repeated scans update one row instead of minting a family.

SCOPE. One replacement only. Do not change how outcomes, answered or episodes are computed, do not touch readAnswersByPanel or the panel matching, and do not alter the existing report fields — only add unanswered alongside them. The emission is fire-and-forget inside try/catch because a reporting resolver must never fail on a write it does not own.

